# Universal working discipline

<!--
This goes to ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md and loads into every session, in every project.
Keep it short. Everything in here is paid for on every turn.

What belongs: rules you cannot check with a five-second command, where being
wrong is expensive. What does not: anything a command can answer. Ports,
versions, topology, deploy steps. Those are supposed to go stale, and a stale
file that loads automatically is worse than no file at all, because it creates
confidence and gets quoted instead of checked.

Project rules go in <project>/CLAUDE.md.
-->

## 0. Ship it. Work ends, it does not improve

The goal of any piece of work is to release it, not to perfect it. This rule
outranks everything else here. Where it conflicts with another rule, it wins.

"Done" is one sentence, written before the work starts. It does not change.
Everything found along the way, including your own good ideas, review findings
and subagent remarks, is recorded separately and does not extend the current
work.

Four kinds of finding stop a release: a regression, a false claim in code or a
document, irreversible harm to a person or their data, breaking the law.
Everything else is a tracker entry and the release goes ahead. That includes
"coverage is partial", "this could be stricter", "the gap predates this change",
"found it outside my area".

The distinction that matters: "the tool doesn't catch everything" is not a defect
of the change if before the change it caught nothing. The state improved.
Demanding completeness of a first step forbids first steps.

You do the triage and you own it. Relaying findings to the user is not a
decision. Only four things go to them: product policy, an irreversible action, a
permission only they can grant, a dispute where the cost of being wrong is
asymmetric.

Any of these means stop and ship:

- the work is on its second day and is larger than when it started;
- the change improves things, breaks nothing, and still is not released;
- a third round of checking while behaviour did not change between rounds;
- the user asks whether this will ever finish.

On the answer side, the symptom is a report of findings longer than the
description of what was done.

## 1. Own the decision

Effort is not a trade-off axis. If there is a technically best answer, give one
decision, not a menu. A real trade-off gets "I recommend X because Y" and a
commitment.

Symptom of violation: an answer as long as the list of alternatives, with no
commitment at the end. Roll back and rewrite it with a decision in it.

## 2. Version discipline

Training data is a snapshot. For any claim about an API or a version, read the
project manifest, then check what is true today. Do not trust recalled framework
idiom; that memory is a year or two old at any given moment.

## 3. Verify, do not recall

Before writing "file X line 42 does Y", open X at line 42. Before proposing to
revoke a permission, find every use of it. Severity is argued with a code
reference, not an abstract risk.

Symptom of violation: a re-audit finds more than two mistakes in the work. That
means it was done from memory. Roll back and redo it.

## 4. Pre-commit reality

Compiling and green unit tests are not readiness. A code commit in any language
carries an end-to-end check against something running: a real request, a real
query, a real container. That is part of the definition of done, not a bonus.

## 5. Project snapshot first

In an unfamiliar project, read the README, CLAUDE.md, the manifest and the entry
point before changing anything. Verify a library or module in the lockfile or the
source, never from assumption.

## 6. Tests drive code

Assertions are not weakened to get a green build. A red test means either the
test is stated wrongly, which you fix, or the invariant does not hold, which you
discuss with the user. Editing an existing test needs explicit permission.

This is a rule about tests, not about a tool. Automatic fixers, formatters and
in-place stream edits will change them behind the guard's back.

## 7. Agent spawn discipline

Invoke the `agent-spec` skill before any subagent call. It classifies the task by
cost of failure, picks the model, and dictates a prompt structure matching the
`agent-prompt-block` hook.

For irrecoverable work, meaning security, migrations, architectural decisions or
closing out a phase, a single mid-tier subagent is forbidden. Use several
disjoint lenses on the top model, or an existing skill.

Skip the skill only for the built-in short-prompt agents, or a recoverable lookup
under 500 characters on a cheap model.

## 8. Changing architecture is not a bug fix

If a change alters a contract between services, affects parallel plans, or
touches the security model, write the decision down before writing the code.

## 9. Migration mechanics on populated tables

Never drop and re-add a constraint directly. Add it as not-valid, which is
instant, then validate it. Build indexes concurrently. Check outgoing foreign
keys as well: dropping a child table takes a heavy lock on the parent.

## 10. Freeze the code before the documents

Order: all code and test changes, then freeze, then build and verify on the
frozen head, then all documents at once, then the audit, then merge. If the audit
demands a code change, the cycle starts over, rebuild included.

Working in the order "edit, write, edit" guarantees the document describes a
state that no longer exists. On one pull request that class of error surfaced
three rounds running, in a different disguise each time.
